{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774144901,"nonce":"nRqP+I0s5EMlTjqu7iz8+HeEQYY=","title":"CRITICAL: Unify chat and history rendering into one shared component library","message":"Chat (StreamRenderer) and History (conversations.tsx) render the same data with different components, leading to persistent visual drift. Every time one is updated, the other falls behind.\n\nTHE FIX IS NOT COSMETIC. The fix is architectural: extract a single shared component library that BOTH views use for rendering messages. The components are:\n- TurnGroup (grouped messages from one entity, sticky badge)\n- MessageBlock (individual block: text, thinking, tool_use)\n- GroupFooter (aggregate stats, always visible)\n- MessageActions (hover overlay with copy, fork, info)\n- Block collapse/expand (thinking blocks, tool use blocks)\n\nBoth views feed their data into these shared components. Chat feeds StreamEvent/RenderNode data. History feeds ConversationBlock data. A thin adapter maps each data source into the shared component props.\n\nMANDATORY CODE COMMENTS at the top of each shared component:\n\"This component is shared between Chat (StreamRenderer) and History (conversations.tsx). Changes here affect BOTH views. If you modify rendering for one view, the other MUST update automatically because they use this same component.\"\n\nThis must never drift again.","files":null}]}